<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samurai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The samurai were the hereditary military class of pre-modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> they became a largely administrative and bureaucratic elite in a long period of peace, paid fixed stipends in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and expected to follow codes of loyalty and service. Commercial growth eroded their economic position, and the class was abolished after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samurai.jpg" alt="Samurai" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samurai_on_horseback0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1609 the Shimazu clan, with the consent of Tokugawa Ieyasu, sent about 3,000 <b>samurai</b> armed with arquebuses in more than 100 ships under Kabayama Hisataka.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The merchant class (chonin), placed at the bottom of the Confucian order beneath <b>samurai</b>, farmers and artisans, accumulated wealth that increasingly outstripped that of the samurai, whose fixed rice stipends were eroded by commercial change; great merchant houses such as Mitsui rose in this period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Japan Confucian ideas arrived with Chinese writing and learning by the 6th century; under the Tokugawa shogunate the Neo-Confucianism of Zhu Xi became the official teaching of the shogunate’s academy and was taught in schools for the <b>samurai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Edo period Japan was governed by a rigid order of inherited rank: the Emperor and his Court at the top, then the shōgun with the rōjū and the daimyō, then the <b>samurai</b>, farmers, artisans and merchants.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In Heian Japan the Fujiwara family dominated the court by marrying its daughters to emperors, and in 1016 Fujiwara no Michinaga became regent for his grandson Emperor Go-Ichijō; later the daimyō, warrior lords with their own <b>samurai</b>, held real power.</blockquote><a href="/government/aristocracy">Aristocracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Japanese quarter, Ban Yipun, under a chief the Thai authorities appointed, held traders, Christians fleeing persecution and former <b>samurai</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
